{"packet_id":"D005-01","doc_id":"D005","jurisdiction":"CA","category":"application_screening_fees","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"California tenant screening fee cap and refund rules","requirement":"A landlord may not charge a prospective tenant more than the annual maximum screening fee, must give a receipt and refund any unused portion, and may not charge a fee when no rental unit is available.","key_value":"$68.96 maximum screening fee for 2026","coverage_conditions":"Prospective tenants charged a screening fee for a residential rental; the cap is the maximum allowed under California Civil Code Section 1950.6.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Applicants screened for residential rentals"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":"2026","citation":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1950.6","quoted_span":"The maximum tenant screening fee for 2026 is\n$68.96\nLandlords may use screening fees to obtain information about a prospective tenant like a consumer credit report.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D005-01","doc_id":"D005","jurisdiction":"Berkeley, CA","category":"application_screening_fees","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Berkeley screening fee disclosure and non-refundable application fee ban","requirement":"Before collecting a screening fee, an owner must give the applicant a tenant screening fee rights statement and the state fee cap; owners may not charge an existing tenant a non-refundable fee to renew a tenancy or change roommates.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Chapter 13.78 applies to all residential rental agreements in Berkeley, regardless of contrary language in any rental agreement or lease.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"All residential rental agreements in Berkeley"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Any provision of an existing rental agreement or lease that violates the chapter is unenforceable.","effective_date":null,"citation":"Berkeley Mun. Code ch. 13.78","quoted_span":"An owner of residential rental property (or the owner’s agent) cannot charge a non-refundable fee to any existing tenant for the purpose of renewing a tenancy, in whole or in part, including any fee associated with the departure of a roommate or to request to add or replace a roommate in a pre-existing household.","interaction":"Adds local disclosure and non-refundable fee rules on top of the state cap in Cal. Civ. Code § 1950.6.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
